<p>As I get older, I need to exercise. I decided to use a <a href="https://threadmil.common-lisp.dev" target="_blank"><strong>Threadmi&lambda;</strong></a>.</p>
  
<p>Of course, this is another rabbit's hole I fell into many years ago and I am getting out of it right now.
  The reason I fell into this specific rabbit's hole is the lack of portable <em>timeout</em> facilities
  in the testing frameworks out there, including my favorite one, <strong>FiveAM</strong>.  More on this later.</p>
  
<p>My solution, NIH-syndrome-inspired of course, was to write a <em>portability</em> layer for <em>timeout</em> facilities.</p>

<p>Which begged the question: why not <strong>Bordeaux Threads</strong>?</p>

<p>The answer is, again, NIH and the fact that <strong>Bordeaux Threads</strong> has undergone some bit rot over the years and that
  it is not fully complete with respect to many of the implementations out there, especially when it comes to the facilities that
  the implementations provide.</p>

<p>The result is <strong>Threadmi&lambda;</strong> (or <strong>threadmi&lambda;</strong>, or <strong>ThreadMIL</strong>,
  or <strong>threadmilambda</strong>...), i.e., a
  <strong>Bordeaux Threads</strong> like library, re-done in <em>MILan</em>; the &lambda; is de rigueur.</p>

<h1>Threadmi&lambda;</h1>
  
<p>As I said, <strong>Threadmi&lambda;</strong> (<strong>Threadmil</strong>) is a fork and a somewhat re-implementation
    of the well known <strong>Bordeaux Threads</strong> library that aims to make it even more CL-centric, <em>expand</em> it and fix the
    semantics of a few constructs, most notably the <em>timeout</em> machinery.</p>
  
<p><strong>Threadmi&lambda;</strong> implements, leveraging the underlying <strong>CL</strong>, the following threading facilities.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Threads</li>
    <li>Locks</li>
    <li>Semaphores</li>
    <li>Condition Variables</li>
    <li>Timers</li>
    <li>Mailboxes</li>
    <li>Barriers</li>
    <li>Atomics</li>
    <li>Timeouts</li>
  </ul>

  <p>These are the facilities available in most <strong>CL</strong>, especially <a href="http://lispworks.com">Lispworks</a>.</p>

